# Security order

Every hardening feature must **degrade gracefully**. If it breaks browser, VPN, files, or dev tools, it needs an explicit opt-out — never a silent failure that makes the user nuke the whole subsystem.

## Ship order (do not skip ahead)

**Now (desktop):** launcher monopoly (`cicada-wofi`), left dock, Waybar, pcmanfm-qt via `cicada-files`, Helium, default-deny scopes, Work-as-UID in Settings, HID-allow USBGuard on live, telemetry off. Wi-Fi available at boot.

**Tier 1 (before "daily driver ready"):** LUKS2 + TPM2-with-passphrase-fallback; sbctl Secure Boot + pacman re-sign hook; WireGuard nftables kill switch (tcpdump-tested); `linux-hardened` + `hardened_malloc` actually preloaded; duress LUKS slot with no timing tell; auto-reboot-when-locked; `docs/network-calls.md` telemetry audit.

**Tier 2:** USBGuard while locked; Helium JIT-off policy (managed/recommended Chromium policy); Flatpak portals with honest docs; Firejail/netns for a short untrusted-app list; restic/borg with a tested restore.

**Tier 3 — do not build:** hardware attestation, MTE, kernel per-app sandbox matching Android.

**Auto-reboot-to-rest must fire before an adversary can inhibit it** (GrayKey Preserve / Inseyets Safeguard). Default 30 minutes locked, `systemctl reboot --force`. A 6–18h AFU window is a known fail against that class. USB inserts are blocked for the whole lock. This is still userspace — not Titan.

Do not claim Cellebrite-matrix “no access” on MBA hardware.
